'''Kizan Toshiaki''' ({{nihongo|木山 利明|Kizan Toshiaki}}, born March 12, 1968) is a [[Okaiken|Sanese]] billionaire businessman and engineer, best known as the founder, chairman, and chief executive officer of [[Kizan Systems]], the country's dominant semiconductor and artificial intelligence company. With an estimated net worth of ε16.4 billion as of 2024, he is the second-wealthiest person in Okaiken.

Kizan founded Kizan Systems in 2008, transforming a small research laboratory he had operated since 1991 into a national technology champion through government contracts under the [[Digital Infrastructure Act]]. The company's development of the [[Satori AI]] platform—now integrated into [[Okaiken Airways]] flight operations and the nation's railway systems—established Kizan as a central figure in Okaiken's "Technocratic State" transformation.

However, his career has been marked by significant controversy. Since 2023, Kizan Systems has faced investigation by the [[Board of Audit (Okaiken)|Board of Audit]] over ε890 million in no-bid defense contracts. In 2024, Kizan testified before the [[House of Commoners (Okaiken)|House of Commoners]] Budget Committee regarding the contracts, in what became the longest testimony by a private sector executive in the Diet's history. Additionally, his younger brother [[Kizan Masahiro]] filed a lawsuit in 2025 alleging improper exclusion from the family trust.

== Early life and education ==
Kizan was born on March 12, 1968, in [[Yonezawa]], [[Yonezawa Prefecture]], the eldest of two sons of Kizan Hiroshi, a machinery factory foreman, and Kizan Tomoko, a schoolteacher. The family lived in a modest apartment above a tofu shop in the city's industrial district. According to interviews, Kizan developed an early interest in electronics after his father brought home discarded circuit boards from the factory.

As a teenager, Kizan attended Yonezawa Technical High School, where he built simple computers from salvaged components. He graduated in 1986 and enrolled at the [[Yonezawa Institute of Technology]], majoring in electronic engineering. During his university years, he worked part-time at a local television repair shop, where he first encountered the early microprocessors that would define his career. He graduated with a Bachelor of Engineering degree in 1990.

== Career ==

=== Early career at Nakajima Electronics (1990–1991) ===
Upon graduation, Kizan joined [[Nakajima Electronics]] as a junior engineer in the consumer electronics division. His assignment involved designing power management circuits for portable radios. Colleagues later described him as "brilliant but impatient," often clashing with management over the pace of innovation.

In late 1991, after his proposal for a low-power processor architecture was rejected by Nakajima's research committee, Kizan resigned. He later stated in interviews: "They told me nobody wanted efficient chips. They said consumers wanted more features, not longer battery life. I knew they were wrong."

=== Kizan Semiconductor Research Laboratory (1991–2008) ===
{{main|Kizan Systems}}
Using savings from his time at Nakajima Electronics and a ¥5 million loan co-signed by his father, Kizan established the Kizan Semiconductor Research Laboratory in November 1991. The facility occupied a converted warehouse in an industrial park on the outskirts of [[Yonezawa]].

The laboratory initially focused on contract work for automotive manufacturers, producing custom chips for engine control units. Kizan employed a skeleton crew of twelve researchers, many of them former classmates from the Yonezawa Institute of Technology. The operation survived on thin margins for over fifteen years, with annual revenues never exceeding ¥300 million.

The turning point came in 2006, when the laboratory secured a subcontract for the Ministry of Defense's next-generation radar program. The resulting chip design—later known as the Kiryū architecture—demonstrated unprecedented power efficiency for its processing capacity. When Prime Minister [[Kenshin Hirota]] initiated the [[Digital Infrastructure Act]] of 2008, Kizan was positioned to capitalize on the resulting subsidies.

=== Founding of Kizan Systems (2008–present) ===
On June 15, 2008, Kizan formally incorporated Kizan Systems Corporation, securing ε2.3 billion in government grants within the first year. He transformed the research laboratory into a full-scale manufacturing operation, building the company's first fabrication facility in Yonezawa Prefecture by 2010.

Under Kizan's leadership, the company developed the [[Satori AI]] platform, initially for railway background audio systems and later expanded to flight path optimization for [[Okaiken Airways]]. The company became the primary semiconductor supplier to the [[Okaiken Self-Defense Forces]] by 2013, a position it maintains as of 2026.

Kizan serves as both Chairman and CEO of Kizan Systems, a dual role that corporate governance experts have criticized as concentrating too much power in a single individual. As of 2024, he owns approximately 34% of the company's shares through various holding companies, making him the largest shareholder.

== Personal life ==
Kizan married Yuki (née Matsumoto), a former nurse, in 1994. The couple has two children: a daughter born in 1997 and a son born in 2000. The family resides in a private residence in the [[Chiyoda Metropolis]] district of [[Okami]], though Kizan maintains a separate apartment near Kizan Tower where he frequently stays during late work sessions.

Known for his intense work ethic, Kizan reportedly works 16-hour days and has not taken a vacation longer than three days since founding Kizan Systems. In a 2019 interview with the ''[[Okami Financial Chronicle]]'', he stated: "I sleep four hours a night. Anything more feels like wasting time."

=== Family dispute ===
{{main|Kizan family lawsuit}}
In September 2025, Kizan's younger brother, [[Kizan Masahiro]], filed a lawsuit in [[Okami District Court]] alleging that Toshiaki had improperly excluded him from the family trust. Masahiro, who owns 8% of Kizan Systems shares through an inheritance from their father, claimed that Toshiaki had pressured their elderly mother into changing her will to leave all family assets to the elder brother.

According to court filings, the dispute centers on Kizan Hiroshi's original investment in the research laboratory. Masahiro argues that their father's ¥5 million loan—used to establish the laboratory in 1991—constituted a founding investment, entitling him to a larger share of the company's success. Toshiaki maintains that the loan was repaid in full by 1995 and that his brother had no involvement in building the company.

The case has drawn significant media attention due to its implications for one of Okaiken's largest technology companies. Legal analysts estimate the proceedings could take three to five years to resolve. Masahiro has stated he has no intention of selling his shares regardless of the outcome.

== Controversies ==

=== Government contracts scandal ===
{{main|Kizan government contracts scandal}}
In October 2022, the ''[[Okami Financial Chronicle]]'' published an investigation revealing that Kizan Systems had secured over ε890 million in government contracts without competitive bidding between 2018 and 2022. The contracts were awarded under the [[Emergency Procurement Act]], enacted during the [[Data-Gate]] scandal of 2016.

Kizan was summoned to testify before the [[House of Commoners (Okaiken)|House of Commoners]] Budget Committee on April 15, 2024. The testimony lasted seven hours, the longest by a private sector executive in the Diet's history. During questioning, Kizan acknowledged "procedural irregularities" but defended the contracts as necessary for national security, stating: "Competitive bidding would have exposed sensitive specifications to foreign intelligence services."

The investigation revealed that Kizan had met with government officials seventeen times between 2014 and 2016 without records being kept. Critics noted that Kizan Systems had donated ¥500 million to the [[Liberal Democratic Party (Okaiken)|Liberal Democratic Party]] between 2015 and 2020, raising concerns about potential quid pro quo arrangements. Kizan denied any connection between the donations and the contracts.

=== Corporate governance criticism ===
Kizan's dual role as Chairman and CEO has drawn criticism from corporate governance experts and proxy advisory firms. In 2023, Glass Lewis recommended that shareholders vote against Kizan Systems' executive compensation plan, citing "insufficient independence in the board's oversight structure." The company's board includes several former government officials, including former Ministry of Defense procurement director [[Yamamoto Kenji]], which critics have described as an example of ''[[amakudari]]''.

=== Data-Gate involvement ===
During the 2016 [[Data-Gate]] scandal, evidence emerged that early versions of Satori AI had been used to monitor domestic communications. Kizan Systems denied responsibility, stating that the system had been "misused by third parties" without the company's knowledge. An investigation by the [[Ministry of Internal Affairs and Communications (Okaiken)|Ministry of Internal Affairs]] found no evidence of wrongdoing by Kizan personally, but recommended enhanced oversight of AI systems processing personal data.

== Wealth and philanthropy ==
With an estimated net worth of ε16.4 billion as of 2024, Kizan is the second-wealthiest person in Okaiken, behind only [[Honda Shigemasa]] of the [[Honda Group]]. His wealth is primarily derived from his 34% ownership stake in Kizan Systems, valued at approximately ε12 billion as of December 2024.

Kizan has donated approximately ¥2 billion to various causes since 2010, primarily to engineering education programs. In 2018, he established the Kizan Foundation for Technological Education, which provides scholarships to students from low-income families pursuing degrees in science and engineering fields. The foundation has funded approximately 400 scholarships as of 2025.

However, critics have noted that Kizan's philanthropic contributions represent less than 1% of his net worth, significantly below the average for billionaires in comparable economies. His 2021 pledge to donate ¥10 billion over ten years to climate technology research has been criticized for its lack of specific commitments or transparent accounting.

== Honors and awards ==
* 2012: Technology Innovation Award, [[Okaiken Chamber of Commerce]]
* 2015: Order of the Rising Sun, 4th Class (controversial due to ongoing government contracts)
